package wasdk:platform@2.3.0;

/// What changes in a folder the program holds, as the system tells it: FSEvents on macOS, inotify
/// on Linux and Android, `ReadDirectoryChangesW` on Windows. A folder the program holds is one it
/// has a descriptor of: its own folders and its mounts, opened from `wasi:filesystem`'s preopened
/// root, the folders the user picked for it, a machine's root and the folders opened from it.
/// Watching is reading, and asks nothing; nothing outside the folder is told.
///
/// **A change names a path relative to the watched folder**, its names joined by `/`, the folder
/// itself being `.`. A watch reports what the system reports, coalesced as the system coalesces
/// it: a file written and closed may be one change or several, and an editor's save may be a file
/// made and renamed onto the one it replaces. A change made shortly before the watch began may
/// still be reported, as FSEvents delivers one late; never the folder's own creation, since the
/// folder was there to be watched. A program that needs to know what is there now reads it.
///
/// **Changes come in batches**, each at most `latency-ms` after the first change in it, so a
/// burst of changes is one batch. A batch the program has not read grows until the system's
/// queue, or WaOS's, would hold too much; then the changes are dropped and a `rescan` takes their
/// place. A rescan of the whole folder is its batch alone: what it reads again holds the rest. A
/// watch ends when the program drops its stream.
interface file-watching {
  use wasi:filesystem/types@0.3.0.{descriptor, error-code};

  /// One change in a watched folder, by its path relative to the folder.
  variant change {
    /// Something was made at the path: a file, a folder or a symlink, or moved there from
    /// outside the folder.
    created(string),
    /// What is at the path was written, or its metadata changed.
    modified(string),
    /// What was at the path is gone, or moved outside the folder.
    removed(string),
    /// What was at the first path is at the second, where the system pairs the two ends of a
    /// rename: inotify and Windows do; FSEvents does not, and tells each end as `moved`.
    renamed(tuple<string, string>),
    /// Something was moved to or from the path, and the system did not say which, or from or
    /// to where: macOS reports each end of a rename on its own. Look at the path to see what is
    /// there now.
    moved(string),
    /// Something changed at the path; the system did not say what.
    other(string),
    /// Changes were lost: what is under the path must be read again, or the whole folder with
    /// none.
    rescan(option<string>),
  }

  /// Watches `folder` until the stream is dropped: its own entries, and with `recursive`
  /// everything under it, a symlink's target never. Resolves once the system watches it, which
  /// for a large folder watched recursively on Linux, where each folder beneath is watched apart,
  /// takes a while. `not-directory` for a file; `unsupported` for a folder above the program's
  /// mounts, the root among them, and on Windows for the root of this computer, the folder of its
  /// drives.
  watch: async func(folder: borrow<descriptor>, recursive: bool, latency-ms: u32) -> result<stream<list<change>>, error-code>;
}
